<asp:Content ID="Content3" ContentPlaceHolderID="ContentPlaceHolder2" runat="server">

    <div id="MainBox">
        <h2>
            Master Roshi, also known as the Turtle Hermit, is one of the most iconic characters in the <br>
            Dragon Ball franchise. As a wise mentor and skilled martial artist, Roshi plays a crucial role <br>
            in shaping the destinies of several key characters, including Goku, Krillin, and Bulma. We'll <br>
            explore his origins, teachings, character traits, and evolution throughout the <br>
            series, ultimately highlighting his significance in the Dragon Ball universe.
        </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="textBoxes" id="box1">
        <h2>
            Master Roshi was first introduced in Dragon Ball as a hermit living on a remote island. He <br>
            is characterized by his long white beard, sunglasses, and distinctive turtle shell. Roshi <br>
            is over 300 years old, which adds to his aura of wisdom and experience. His long life has <br>
            allowed him to become one of the most powerful martial artists in the world, and his extensive <br>
            knowledge of martial arts techniques and philosophy makes him a revered figure.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box2">
        <h2>
            Roshi's backstory reveals that he trained alongside other legendary figures, including the <br>
            Ox-King and the West Kai. He earned the title of "Turtle Hermit" after saving a turtle from a <br>
            hunter, showcasing his compassionate nature. This incident also led him to create the <br>
            Kamehameha wave, one of the most iconic energy attacks in the series. Roshi’s character is a <br>
            mix of humor and seriousness, serving as both a mentor and a source of comic relief.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box3">
        <h2>
            In Dragon Ball, Master Roshi takes on the role of a mentor to Goku and Krillin. He begins <br>
            training them for the Tenkaichi Budokai (World Martial Arts Tournament), where they learn <br>
            valuable skills and techniques. Roshi's teaching methods, while often humorous and <br>
            unconventional, are effective. He emphasizes hard work, discipline, and the importance of <br>
            martial arts in personal development.
        </h2>
        <img id="RoshiTraining1" src="/images/RoshiTraining1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box4">
        <h2>
            Roshi's training regimen includes rigorous physical activities, such as carrying heavy <br>
            objects, which builds strength and endurance. His lessons go beyond just fighting techniques; <br>
            he imparts philosophical wisdom that shapes Goku and Krillin's character development. One of <br>
            the most notable lessons is the importance of perseverance, exemplified when Goku and Krillin <br>
            undergo grueling training routines.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box5">
        <h2>
            Roshi’s involvement in the Tenkaichi Budokai showcases his skills as a fighter and his <br>
            competitive spirit. Under the alias "Jackie Chun," he enters the tournament to test his <br>
            students and prevent them from becoming overconfident. This decision underscores his dedication <br>
            to their growth and his desire to remain humble despite his immense power. 
        </h2>
        <img id="JackieChan1" src="/images/JackieChan1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box6">
        <h2>
            During the tournament, Roshi faces off against powerful opponents, demonstrating his expertise <br>
            and cunning. His fight with Goku in the final round is a memorable moment that illustrates the <br>
            bond between teacher and student. Roshi ultimately loses but passes on his knowledge and <br>
            fighting spirit to Goku, reinforcing the theme of mentorship.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box7">
        <h2>
            As Dragon Ball Z progresses, Master Roshi's role shifts significantly. The arrival of more <br>
            powerful characters, especially the Saiyans, emphasizes his limitations as a fighter. While <br>
            still a respected figure, Roshi often finds himself overshadowed by younger warriors like Goku, <br>
            Vegeta, and Piccolo. Despite this, he continues to provide support and guidance to the Z Fighters.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box8">
        <h2>
            Roshi’s character reflects a theme of aging and the acceptance of one’s place in the world. <br>
            While he may not be the strongest fighter anymore, his wisdom and experience are invaluable. <br>
            He becomes a voice of reason and strategy during battles, helping to devise plans and offering <br>
            insights into opponents’ abilities. His interactions with the newer generation of fighters <br>
            highlight the importance of mentorship across generations.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box9">
        <h2>
            In the Majin Buu Saga, Master Roshi makes a notable return to the battlefield, demonstrating <br>
            that age does not diminish his combat skills entirely. During the battle against Majin Buu, <br>
            Roshi showcases his fighting abilities and uses his knowledge to strategize against their <br>
            formidable enemy. His involvement serves as a reminder that even older fighters can still <br>
            contribute significantly to the fight.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box10">
        <h2>
            One of the most memorable moments occurs when Roshi uses the Kamehameha wave to assist in the <br>
            battle against Buu. This scene reaffirms Roshi’s enduring spirit and commitment to protect <br>
            Earth, despite the odds. It highlights that while he may not be the strongest fighter, his <br>
            heart and determination remain unyielding.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box11">
        <h2>
            Master Roshi is also known for his comedic traits, often engaging in humorous antics that <br>
            provide comic relief. His infamous perversions and flirtations with women create a lighthearted <br>
            tone, contrasting with the serious battles and challenges the characters face. This humor is not <br>
            just for laughs; it serves to humanize Roshi and makes him relatable, showing that even wise <br>
            mentors have flaws and desires.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box12">
        <h2>
            Roshi’s character is multidimensional; while he embodies the archetype of the wise old master, <br>
            he also has moments of vulnerability and growth. His playful nature often leads to comedic <br>
            situations, but beneath the surface, he carries the weight of his long life and the lessons he <br>
            has learned. His occasional struggles with loneliness and the passing of time add depth to his <br>
            character, making him more relatable to the audience.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box13">
        <h2>
            In Dragon Ball Super, Master Roshi's role continues to evolve, especially during the Universe <br>
            Survival Saga. Despite his age, he is called upon to participate in the Tournament of Power, <br>
            showcasing his resilience and enduring skills. This tournament highlights his unique abilities <br>
            and strategic mind, even among younger, more powerful fighters.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box14">
        <h2>
            Roshi surprises everyone by utilizing the Mafuba (Evil Containment Wave) technique during the <br>
            tournament, demonstrating that he is still a formidable fighter despite his old age. His <br>
            participation emphasizes the importance of experience and strategy over raw power. The moment <br>
            he successfully utilizes the Mafuba to attempt to seal away powerful opponents adds depth to <br>
            his character, showcasing that he can still contribute meaningfully in high-stakes situations.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box15">
        <h2>
            Master Roshi's legacy in the Dragon Ball franchise is immense. He has influenced numerous <br>
            characters, teaching them not only martial arts techniques but also life lessons. His impact <br>
            is felt throughout the series, serving as a bridge between the original Dragon Ball and the <br>
            newer installments.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box16">
        <h2>
            Roshi's character embodies the theme of mentorship and the idea that wisdom can come from the <br>
            most unexpected places. His eccentricity and humor continue to resonate with fans, making him <br>
            a memorable character who stands the test of time.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box17">
        <h2>
            Master Roshi is a multifaceted character whose contributions to the Dragon Ball series extend <br>
            far beyond his martial arts skills. He represents the essence of mentorship, wisdom, and the <br>
            enduring spirit of martial arts. His role in Dragon Ball Super reaffirms his significance <br>
            within the franchise, demonstrating that even the oldest characters can have a vital role in a <br>
            world filled with immense power and new challenges. Through his quirks and wisdom, Master <br>
            Roshi remains an integral part of the Dragon Ball legacy, captivating fans with his unique <br>
            charm and lasting impact on the series.
        </h2>
    </div>

